15 out of every 100 people in the uk  have a disability but just 4 in 100 have  a blue badge it's the scheme that allows  you to park on yellow lines and in  parking places for as long as you need  it  and if you just step back  okay and round  and let's tuck me up nice and toasty but  disability campaigners say some city  centers are squeezing them out  in york there are new security barriers  keeping out all vehicles from a larger  area of the city center  i think the fact that there are so many  people  it sparked protests from blue badge  holders who've lost access to more shops  and services  many of us can't walk  very far down that street or  for example today i have a wheelchair i  have my carer but i don't always have  that and if i want to go out with  friends i  need to be able to park so close that i  can walk the 10 metres that i can walk  to go to the place i'm going to  [Music]  christmas markets have drawn crowds at  the end of another challenging year for  shoppers and traders  but campaigners say some councils across  the country have reduced disabled  parking during the pandemic  and fear york's permanent changes could  be copied elsewhere  almost like an apartheid situation i do  think it's that serious and i think  other towns and cities will actually  look at how to keep their city centres  more car free  but disabled people need to be at the  table when those discussions are  happening  york city council says it needs the new  restrictions to keep people safe from  potential terror attacks and that it  acted on clear police advice it's  pledged to improve facilities for the  less mobile and appoint an access  officer the protesters are not convinced  york's lack of access could yet end up  in court campaigners have crowdfunded  more than 5 000 pounds and hope to  challenge the council under the equality  act  they see the new barriers as simple  discrimination  in the meantime they remain closed from  10 30 every morning gerard sky news york
 

